    "summary": "After Hjumpik helped Thistle and Thorn adjust a magical tree, the guards realized he was an intruder and demanded a formal duel. Hjumpik used his warhammer to shock the ground and unbalance Thistle, forcing a yield. Waluigi notes that winning a duel against a fey knight without killing them is the quickest way to become a 'supervised nuisance' rather than a 'corpse.'",
    "description": "The duel was conducted under \"Fey Jurisdictional Law.\" Thistle used a high-dexterity blade-style designed to subdue, but Hjumpik's raw, \"brutish\" strength was a mismatch for the guard's refined movements. By striking the ground rather than the man, Hjumpik utilized the earth's resonance to disrupt Thistle's footing. The rakshasa Zephyrclaws was seen taking bets from the local sprites during the fight.\n\n## How The Duel Began\n\nThe confrontation was not sought. After Hjumpik helped the fey pair Thistle and Thorn adjust a magical tree, the grove's guards realized he was an intruder rather than an invited hand and demanded a formal duel in response. This is the Feyward Grove's version of due process: trespass is not punished by execution but adjudicated by single combat under Fey Jurisdictional Law, which suits a place where time runs on a loop of bliss and revelry and everything, including justice, is a bit of a performance.\n\n## The Mismatch\n\nThe fight was a study in incompatible fighting philosophies. Thistle, a fey knight, moved with high-dexterity precision, wielding a subduing blade-style built to disarm and humble an opponent without killing—elegant, refined, and entirely optimized for fighting other elegant, refined opponents. Hjumpik, a dwarf with a warhammer and an exhausting commitment to doing the right thing, was none of those things. His raw strength did not fit inside Thistle's assumptions, and refined movement has no answer for a man who refuses to be where the blade expects him to be.\n\n## The Winning Blow\n\nHjumpik's decisive move was not aimed at Thistle at all. Rather than strike the knight, he struck the ground, using the earth's resonance to disrupt Thistle's footing and force a yield. The choice mattered as much as the result: by winning without killing, Hjumpik stayed inside the grove's rules and preserved a fey knight's life. Meanwhile the rakshasa Zephyrclaws worked the crowd, taking bets from the local sprites—because in the Feyward Grove even a lawful duel is, first and foremost, entertainment.\n\n## Aftermath\n\nWaluigi notes that winning a duel against a fey knight without killing them is the quickest way to become a \"supervised nuisance\" rather than a \"corpse.\" Hjumpik's victory did not free him from the grove's scrutiny so much as upgrade his status within it—from intruder to be removed to oddity to be watched. In the Feywild, that is roughly the best outcome an honest dwarf can hope for. WAH.",
    "aftermath": "Thistle and Thorn agreed to keep Hjumpik's presence a secret from the 'boss' (the Orange Oracle). This win secured Hjumpik a temporary pass to move through the grove, though the screaming cowbell quickly ended his chances of being stealthy.",
