    "date": "Day 30, 1040 BF (Afternoon)",
    "location": "Raventree Manor — The Interior Auction Gallery",
    "type": "Melee / Multi-Faction Chaos / Rescue Operation (Unofficial)",
    "result": "Remi freed — Mystic Morsole escaped through a window — Mages Guild operative withdrew — Wario appeared — Markop was shoulder-barged into the floor and bled on it",
    "belligerents": {
      "attackers": {
        "name": "Disaster Inc. and associated chaos",
        "factionId": "disaster_inc",
        "commander": "Markop (window-breaker, de facto initiator); Bowser (designated to burn things down)"
      },
      "defenders": {
        "name": "Everyone who thought they owned Remi",
        "factionId": "multiple_hostile",
        "commander": "Mystic Morsole (winning bidder); Mages Guild Operative (competing claim); Wario (arrived for unknown reasons and punched Markop)"
      }
    },
    "casualties": {
      "attackers": "Markop — shoulder-barged by Wario, fell bleeding; general dignity of party — moderate damage",
      "defenders": "Mystic Morsole — tactical window exit, uninjured; Mages Guild operative — withdrew; Wario — unharmed because Wario is always unharmed"
    },
    "summary": "The brawl began the moment Markop broke the window overlooking the auction room. What had been a covert observation position became an entry point in an instant, and the entry was not subtle. Wario was already inside, or arrived with the speed that suggests Wario had been monitoring the situation from an independent position. A Mages Guild operative — whose organizational affiliation suggests this was not opportunistic but coordinated — immediately asserted a competing claim on Remi. Bowser prepared to burn everything. Markop summoned a moonbeam. Mystic Morsole exited through a secondary window with practiced precision. Remi ran and Dan caught her. The fight was chaotic, three-sided, and brief, which is the only kind of fight Raventree Manor seems to permit.",
    "description": "Waluigi is documenting this engagement as a battle entry despite its brevity because the PARTICIPANTS and IMPLICATIONS deserve the full treatment.\n\n## The Window (Markop's Decision)\n\nMarkop had been observing the auction through a window. Through that window he watched the bid complete, watched Morsole claim Remi as purchased property, watched Remi — alive again but standing next to a man who had just spent twelve thousand gold on the right to say he owned her. And Markop broke the window.\n\nWaluigi has thought about this decision. Waluigi has considered the alternatives — waiting, negotiating, finding another entry point, sending a messenger, any of the diplomatic options that exist in theory. And Waluigi has concluded: Markop was correct. Sometimes the window needs to be broken. Sometimes the correct tactical choice and the correct MORAL choice align perfectly and they both say BREAK THE WINDOW.\n\n## The Three-Sided Problem\n\nWhat the party did not anticipate — what could not have been anticipated from an observation position — was that the auction room contained more interested parties than just the winning bidder. The moment Markop broke the window and Bowser charged in declaring his intent to burn things, two additional complications manifested.\n\nFirst: Wario. Wario, who the party had reasons to believe might be dead, arrived with a fake mustache and the casual violence of someone who had been waiting for exactly this situation. He shoulder-barged Markop to the floor. Markop bled. Wario established his position in the room and told Remi to come with him.\n\nSecond: a Mages Guild operative. Not an opportunist — an operative. Someone dispatched specifically, which means the Mages Guild knew about the auction, knew Remi would be there, and had sent a representative prepared to extract her by force if the bidding failed. The operative stabbed — or moved to stab — and made their own claim.\n\nThree separate parties. Three separate claims on one person. All arriving at the same point simultaneously. Waluigi notes that this is not coincidence. Three factions do not independently converge on the same auction without intelligence networks that overlap in ways nobody has fully mapped. Someone knew Remi would be here. Multiple someones. The stone auction was not random. Remi being in that room was KNOWN about in advance by entities with very different agendas and very similar methods of information gathering.\n\nWaluigi has written this down. Waluigi intends to return to this point when he has more data.\n\n## Bowser's Contribution (The Threat That Worked)\n\nBowser entered the room prepared to burn it. He said so explicitly. He pulled out a device to call his son for reinforcement — Koopa reinforcement, which is a specific category of assistance that tends to resolve situations by making them significantly larger before making them smaller. He planted himself in the middle of the chaos and radiated the specific energy of someone who has DECIDED.\n\nWaluigi believes Bowser's intervention — the declaration, the son-calling device, the physical presence of a Koopa King who had already said the word 'burn' — contributed materially to Mystic Morsole's window exit and the Mages Guild operative's withdrawal. Nobody wanted to be in a room that Bowser had committed to burning. Not even Wario, who has survived a remarkable number of dangerous situations, positioned himself away from Bowser during the brawl.\n\nThe threat of overwhelming force, credibly delivered, shortened a fight that could have been significantly longer.\n\n## Markop's Moonbeam (Noted for the Record)\n\nIn the middle of being on the floor and bleeding, Markop summoned a moonbeam. Waluigi documents this because it is the kind of detail that reveals CHARACTER. The man had been shoulder-barged into the ground by Wario, was bleeding, had just broken a window, was in a room with three hostile factions, and his response was to summon divine light. Not to flee. Not to negotiate. To summon a moonbeam and make the room WORSE for everyone who shouldn't be there.\n\nWaluigi respects this. Waluigi would not do it himself — Waluigi's approach to being knocked down involves getting up, which is different — but he respects it.\n\n## Salam Picked Flowers\n\nWaluigi does not understand this. Waluigi is documenting it because it happened and the historical record should be complete. During the fight, Salam picked flowers. Waluigi does not know what flowers were available in the auction gallery of a haunted manor. Waluigi does not know what purpose they served. Waluigi is going to let this one go because some mysteries are not worth the investigation.\n\n## Resolution and Exit\n\nRemi ran. Dan tried to grab her first and failed — Dan frequently fails at grabbing Remi, this is a documented pattern — but when Remi chose to go to Dan, she did so willingly and with apparent relief. The auction was over. The room had served its purpose and now everyone wanted to be somewhere else.\n\nMystic Morsole was already outside waiting. Wario was already crossing items off his debt list. The Mages Guild operative had vanished without a documented exit, which is the kind of thing operatives do and is itself worth noting — they knew when to withdraw and they did so cleanly. Nobody pursued them. Nobody had the capacity.\n\nThe brawl lasted minutes. Its consequences are ongoing.",
    "aftermath": "Remi was free but Mystic Morsole was attached to the group. Wario was present with a debt list and renewed claims on Bowser's finances. The Mages Guild had demonstrated operational interest in Remi's location and status. Markop had a floor wound and had been right about breaking the window. The moonbeam, presumably, faded. Salam had flowers.",
