    "summary": "The party engaged a Shard Stalker inside a solarium filled with mirrors. The monster bounced between reflections, creating an infinitely frustrating game of whack-a-mole. Bowser punched the glass until his knuckles bled. Hjumpik was pulled into the mirror realm, faced a blue copy of himself, and eventually, a 'Red Hjumpik' stepped out into the real world. Waluigi officially declares mirrors to be the worst invention in history.",
    "description": "Waluigi hates reflections. They are never as handsome as the original. But in Raventree Manor, reflections actively try to kill you.\n\n**The Engagement**\nThe Shard Stalker teleported between the dozen free-standing mirrors in the ruined Solarium, attacking from impossible angles. Archie, showing incredible restraint, threw flaming twigs and controlled bonfires instead of his signature Fireball, earning nods from the Mages' Guild observers. Dan, despite having only one functional arm, swung his axe at the glass while Toad Lee tried to coordinate defensive formations.\n\n**The Mirror Realm**\nBowser, furious that the monster was using trickery, began physically punching the mirrors, shattering them with his bare hands. During the chaos, Hjumpik was pulled THROUGH the glass into a floating, inverse library. There, he encountered a 'Blue Hjumpik' that warned him: 'If you strike me, you strike yourself.'\n\n**The Red Emergence**\nBack in the physical world, as the final mirrors shattered, a 'Red Hjumpik' stepped out. He looked identical, but his demeanor was... off. He smiled too much. The Mages' Guild, swooping in like the bureaucratic vultures they are, captured the remains of the Shard Stalker in a null cage, turning it to stone. They also demanded to study the Red Hjumpik. \n\nWaluigi watched from a safe distance. The party survived, but they are now traveling with a version of their friend who might just be a reflection wearing his skin. Waluigi is keeping one hand on his tennis racket at all times.",
    "aftermath": "The Mages' Guild claimed the petrified monster, removing evidence of the anomaly. The Oracle, annoyed that the monster wasn't truly 'killed,' ushered the party toward the final Summoning Room, noting that the ritual would now require blood to complete.",
