    "summary": "The moment Vivian Corvinarus stopped pretending to be a harmless thrall named Vivesna and started hunting party members through the Shadow Estate's corridors. Markop woke up being DRAGGED by Bowser because the Corvinarus succession claimant was chasing them with an axe and magic that shouldn't work in the Shadowfell but DID because the Corvinarus bloodline apparently doesn't follow dimensional rules. Markop survived a crimson death beam only because he whispered a prayer at exactly the right moment. Bowser found a necromancer's wand and asked 'Is this a magic wand?' Waluigi wasn't there but the secondhand account made him grateful for the Feywild's fairy politics, which is something Waluigi NEVER expected to feel.",
    "description": "Waluigi is reconstructing this fight from Markop's account. Markop does not exaggerate. When Markop says something was desperate, Waluigi translates that as 'we were seconds from death and only survived through luck, divine intervention, and Bowser being Bowser.'\n\n## Phase 1: The Drag\n\nMarkop regained consciousness while being hauled across stone floors by his tail. Bowser was dragging him through dim corridors while footsteps echoed behind them—Vivian's footsteps, measured and unhurried, because Vivian apparently felt no need to RUN when pursuing prey through a manor he'd spent weeks mapping from the inside as 'Vivesna.'\n\nThis is the advantage of the triple identity. Vivian had been a THRALL in this estate. He knew the corridors. He knew the patrol routes. He knew which doors locked and which didn't, which hallways dead-ended and which connected. Markop and Bowser were running blind in enemy territory. Vivian was hunting in his own HOME.\n\nMarkop managed to push himself upright enough to ask 'What happened?' Bowser's answer was characteristically direct: 'Running away from that guy.' Bowser had held Vivian off with fire breath earlier, buying enough time to drag Markop to temporary safety.\n\n## Phase 2: The Maintenance Room\n\nThey barricaded in a groundskeeper's maintenance room. Waluigi notes the strange details: among the gardening tools and clay pots were ordinary vegetables. NORMAL vegetables. Not shadow-formed, not residue-covered. Carrots and whatever else grows in haunted vampire manor gardens. In a dimension of death and entropy, someone had been doing AGRICULTURE.\n\nThis strikes Waluigi as oddly significant. The Shadow Estate isn't just a vampire court—it's a functioning household with servants, groundskeepers, and vegetable gardens. It's ALIVE in ways that the death dimension it occupies shouldn't allow. Someone is maintaining normalcy here. Whether that's comforting or terrifying depends on perspective.\n\nBowser braced the door with his full weight. Bowser weighs approximately as much as several large vehicles. The door held for about thirty seconds.\n\n## Phase 3: The Breach\n\nVivian hacked through the door with an axe. Not magic—STEEL. While posing as Vivesna the thrall, Vivian had presumably had access to the estate's tool rooms, weapon stores, and service corridors. He knew where the axes were. He'd probably catalogued them.\n\nThe methodical axe strikes demonstrated something important: Vivian doesn't NEED magic. He's trained with physical weapons. He adapts his approach to circumstances. When the dimension suppresses spellcasting, he picks up an axe. When circumstances favor magic, he casts a death beam. This tactical flexibility makes him significantly more dangerous than a pure mage or a pure fighter.\n\nWhen the door burst open, Vivian entered with the calm of someone walking into a room he owns. Smoke bomb first—thick, blinding cloud. Professional entry technique. Then advance through the concealment.\n\nMarkop deflected the smoke and pushed past Vivian into the corridor. Bowser struck from the side—a solid hit across Vivian's head that staggered him. Markop followed with his battle axe, cutting into Vivian's side through the red robes.\n\nVivian bled. Vivian LAUGHED.\n\nWaluigi has discussed this with Markop and confirmed: the laugh was not manic or unhinged. It was AMUSED. Like someone who had been inconvenienced rather than injured. The Corvinarus bloodline's relationship with physical damage is apparently very different from normal biology.\n\n## Phase 4: The Crimson Beam\n\nVivian dropped to one knee. Not from pain—from PREPARATION. Chanting began. Magic gathered with an intensity that the Shadowfell's suppression field should have prevented.\n\n'The Shadowfell suppresses magic,' Vivian said. Then, with a smile that Markop described as 'the most unsettling thing in a dimension full of vampires': 'But not this magic.'\n\nThe crimson beam nearly ended Markop. Full force, overwhelming impact, armor at structural failure. A whispered prayer to his god produced a divine shield that absorbed JUST ENOUGH to keep him standing. The margin between survival and death was measured in faith.\n\nThis is the moment the Shadow Estate became a fundamentally different kind of threat. Magic that ignores planar suppression. A combatant who was invisible for weeks as a thrall. The Corvinarus succession isn't just political theater—it's WARFARE, conducted by someone with capabilities nobody prepared for because nobody knew Vivian HAD them.\n\n## Phase 5: Component Destruction\n\nMarkop's combat instincts saved the moment. He scattered and burned Vivian's spell components—the physical materials needed to channel the crimson beam. Without components, the attack couldn't be easily repeated.\n\nBowser found the necromancer's wand on the ground and picked it up with innocent curiosity. 'Ooooh. Is this a magic wand?' Markop, still recovering from near-death, managed: 'I'm not sure.'\n\nRemi later confirmed: necromancer's wand. Cold to the touch. Death-aspected. The kind of focus that channels efficiently in the Shadowfell's death-energy environment. Whatever Vivian's magic draws from, necromantic tools are part of the toolkit.\n\nVivian withdrew. Not defeated—his injuries didn't seem to bother him enough for that. Not victorious—his primary weapon had been neutralized. He withdrew because the engagement had served its purpose.\n\nWaluigi believes that purpose was DEMONSTRATION. Vivian wasn't trying to kill Markop and Bowser in that corridor. He was trying to prove he COULD.\n\nThe message received. WAH.",
    "aftermath": "Vivian retreated into the Shadow Estate's depths, presumably reverting to another identity or finding a new hiding spot. His weeks as 'Vivesna' gave him intimate knowledge of every corner of the manor. The necromancer's wand is now in party hands—a potential asset if anyone can safely wield it, a liability if it's tracked or cursed. Markop's armor is barely functional. The revelation that Vivian can cast through Shadowfell suppression has rewritten the threat assessment for everyone trapped in this dimension. The Corvinarus succession is no longer a background political drama. It's an active hunting ground, and Vivian knows the territory better than anyone alive.",
