    "summary": "A brief but revealing skirmish during the Shadow Estate pursuit. Rattles held a hellhound, Markop attempted a coordinated flank, Eager panicked and missed, Remi missed two arrows, and Dan clumsily struck hard enough to make the beast flee. The fight ended when knights and Lyranth arrived demanding the Black Crystal from the wrong target.",
    "description": "Waluigi is classifying the hellhound engagement as a skirmish rather than a full battle because the fight was brief, messy, and interrupted by larger pursuit forces. That does not make it unimportant. Small fights reveal habits. This one revealed several.\n\nRattles engaged first, proving again that the skeleton's comic timing should not obscure his willingness to stand in front of danger. Markop attempted to coordinate a two-sided strike with Eager, using himself as the reliable pressure point and Eager as the second angle. The plan was tactically sound. Eager's nerves were not. Instead of closing, he threw a simple dagger, missed, and announced the plan's death with a metallic clink.\n\nRemi opened an empty room because curiosity remains one of her primary movement modes. Markop called her back. She fired two arrows and missed by an impressive margin, which Waluigi attributes to chase stress, bad angles, and the fact that the Shadow Estate had spent the last several minutes turning her life into musical theater.\n\nDan began by tending Eager, then was ordered into the actual fight. His approach was clumsy. His swing was not. The hellhound screamed and fled into another room. This is the rare case where poor form still produced a useful tactical result.\n\nThe skirmish ended not because the party achieved clean control, but because knights and Lyranth arrived and converted the fight into a pursuit. The important outcome was not the hellhound's defeat. It was that the party remained mobile long enough to reach the trophy room and then Tymnas's cottage.\n\nWaluigi's tactical conclusion: Markop remains the group's stabilizing combat thinker; Rattles is more reliable than his bones imply; Eager requires courage training or fewer hellhounds; Remi under chase pressure loses accuracy; Dan's clumsiness can still land if pointed in the correct direction.\n\nWAH.",
